{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 21,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.306,
            0.085,
            0.4,
            0.102
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Solution"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.304,
            0.102,
            0.897,
            0.154
          ],
          "content": "**The total energy consumed by the refrigerator in 30 days would be 400 W** × **8.0 hour/day** × **30 days = 96000 W h = 96 kW h**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.154,
            0.87,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": "**Thus the cost of energy to operate the refrigerator for 30 days is 96 kW h** × **Rs 3.00 per kW h = Rs 288.00**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.224,
            0.75,
            0.254
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Q U E S T I O N S"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.269,
            0.741,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": "**1. What determines the rate at which energy is delivered by a current?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.293,
            0.755,
            0.327
          ],
          "content": "**2. An electric motor takes 5 A from a 220 V line. Determine the power of the motor and the energy consumed in 2 h.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.371,
            0.516,
            0.4
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What you have learnt"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.417,
            0.87,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": "A stream of electrons moving through a conductor constitutes an electric current. **Conventionally, the direction of current is taken opposite to the direction of flow of electrons.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.471,
            0.512,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "The SI unit of electric current is ampere."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.491,
            0.869,
            0.527
          ],
          "content": "To set the electrons in motion in an electric circuit, we use a cell or a battery. A cell **generates a potential difference across its terminals. It is measured in volts (V).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.97,
            0.552,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.528,
            0.869,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": "Resistance is a property that resists the flow of electrons in a conductor. It controls **the magnitude of the current. The SI unit of resistance is ohm (**Ω**).**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.565,
            0.869,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "Ohm’s law: The potential difference across the ends of a resistor is directly **proportional to the current through it, provided its temperature remains the same.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.603,
            0.871,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "The resistance of a conductor depends directly on its length, inversely on its area of **cross-section, and also on the material of the conductor.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.639,
            0.871,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": "n **The equivalent resistance of several resistors in series is equal to the sum of their individual resistances.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.677,
            0.87,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": "A set of resistors connected in parallel has an equivalent resistance $R _ { p }$ **given by**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.705,
            0.353,
            0.743
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ \\frac{1}{R_{p}} = \\frac{1}{R_{1}} + \\frac{1}{R_{2}} + \\frac{1}{R_{3}} + \\cdots \\right.",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.757,
            0.661,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": "The electrical energy dissipated in a resistor is given by"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.799,
            0.869,
            0.834
          ],
          "content": "n **The unit of power is watt (W). One watt of power is consumed when 1 A of current flows at a potential difference of 1 V.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.836,
            0.712,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "The commercial unit of electrical energy is kilowatt hour (kWh)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.853,
            0.473,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "**1 kW h = 3,600,000 J = 3.6** × **10**<strong><sup>6</sup></strong> **J.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
